\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness}

The 455 panels comprise all $\binom{15}{3}$ triples of 15 judges. Each judge therefore appears in $\binom{14}{2} = 91$ panels, and each pair of judges co-occurs in 13. This shared structure induces correlated residuals in the OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}), causing OLS to underestimate standard errors by a factor of approximately $2.5$. Table~\ref{tab:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections that account for this non-independence.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{dir.} & $\boldsymbol{\eta}$\textbf{:}$\boldsymbol{K}$ \\
\midrule
OLS                   & 11/12 & 8/12 & 7+/1$-$ & 1.4:1 \\
Clustered SE (CR1)    & 8/12  & 0/12 & ---     & ---   \\
Mixed effects         & 7/12  & 3/12 & 3+/0$-$ & 2.3:1 \\
Wild cluster bootstrap & 5/12  & 1/12 & 1+/0$-$ & 5:1   \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Robustness of regression results to non-independence correction. Clustered SE uses 3-position max-SE with CR1 correction (13 clusters per position). Mixed effects uses 3-position max-$p$ with random judge intercepts. Wild cluster bootstrap uses $B{=}9999$ resamples, Rademacher weights, and max-$p$ across 3 panel positions. $\eta$:$K$ ratio = $\eta_{\max}$ significant conditions / $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant conditions.}
\label{tab:robustness}
\end{table}

$\eta_{\max}$ remains a robust predictor across all correction methods: 8/12 under clustered standard errors, 7/12 under mixed effects, and 5/12 under the most conservative wild cluster bootstrap. The bootstrap attrition concentrates in ARC conditions (1/6 survive vs.\ MMLU 4/6), likely reflecting lower statistical power in ARC's 2-choice format.

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ collapses under correction (Figure~\ref{fig:robustness}). OLS yields 8/12 FDR-significant conditions (7 positive, 1 negative), but clustered standard errors reduce this to 0/12 and wild cluster bootstrap to 1/12. Point estimates remain predominantly positive, but confidence intervals contain zero in 11 of 12 conditions under bootstrap. The $\eta_{\max}$:$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance ratio shifts from 1.4:1 under OLS to 5:1 under bootstrap: $\eta_{\max}$ robustly predicts panel vulnerability, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s independent contribution is not statistically distinguishable from zero after correcting for shared judge structure.

The sole negative OLS coefficient (ARC $\times$ sycophancy, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.192$) does not survive correction (clustered $p = 0.441$, bootstrap $p \approx 0.06$).

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
